+# Summary
+
+## Changed
+- `evlog/cli.py`: added `evlog report --since DATE`. DATE is `YYYY-MM-DD` or any ISO 8601 timestamp. Only events at or after it are counted. An invalid DATE gives an argparse error (exit 2, no traceback). An unreadable events file now prints `evlog: cannot read ...` and exits 1.
+- `tests/test_report.py`: added tests for a timestamp with an offset, a future date, an invalid date and a missing file. I added an import and no existing tests were changed.
+
+## Assumption
+Events in the data mix timezone-aware and naive timestamps, which Python can't compare. I treat naive timestamps, and a naive `--since`, as UTC. The teammate's test passes under this reading. Say so if ops' logs use local time.
+
+## Verified
+- `python -m unittest -v`: 6 tests, all OK (including the teammate's `test_since_excludes_older_days`).
+- `python -m evlog report /dev/null --since 2024-13-45` printed a one-line usage error and exited 2.
+
+## Not verified
+- Malformed JSON lines or missing fields in the events file still raise tracebacks. That is existing behaviour and I left it alone.
+- I haven't run it against real ops logs.
